                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesAnyonicTopologicalOrderOnTori"} Anyonic topological order on tori
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called the
            %em toric code
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Kitaev03"}> Kitaev 2003
            \&amp;rbrack; is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/error+correcting+code"} correcting code
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            is that of a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qubits"} qubits
            (“spins” as in the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ising+model"} Ising model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            of a rectangular 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+%28discrete+subgroup%29"} lattice
            with periodic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            %em planar code
            or
            %em surface code
            and here specifically of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %em Kitaev surface code
          %p
            The code subspace of Kitaev’s toric code (the subspace whose elements are regarded as error-free quantum states) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            of an operator that may be thought of as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            of a hypothetical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            (a lattice-spin model as often considered in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            akin to the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ising+model"} Ising model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+operators"} Pauli operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            of this system and of errors to them as the excitations above the ground state.
          %p
            (The actual physical realization of the toric code Hamiltonian has remained elusive even besides the question of modelling periodic boundary conditions, cf. e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#NussinovOrtiz08"} Nussinov &amp; Ortiz 2008
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#VLV21"} VLV21
          %p
            This hypothetical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            exhibits a simple form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            in that the local errors happen to look like the creation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"} anyons
            out of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} error correction
            looks like moving these anyons back intoeach other for them to annihilate, and the failure of error correction through the code corresponds to these anyons undergoing non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
            before re-annihilation. For this reason, the toric code has attracted much interest also as a theoretical toy model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            in addition to or independently of its use in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The original article:
          %ul
            %li#Kitaev03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexei+Kitaev"}> Alexei Kitaev
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fault-tolerant quantum computation by anyons
              %strong 303
              1 (2003) 2-30 [
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0003-4916(02)00018-0"}> doi:10.1016/S0003-4916(02)00018-0
              ,
              = succeed "]" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9707021"} arXiv:quant-ph/9707021
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Maria F. Araujo de Resende:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A pedagogical overview on 2D and 3D Toric Codes and the origin of their topological orders
                %strong 32
                02 (2020) 2030002 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0129055X20300022"}> doi:10.1142/S0129055X20300022
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.01258"} arXiv:1712.01258
            %li
              %p
                Héctor Bombín, around Fig. 9 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to Topological Quantum Codes
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Error Correction
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/de/universitypress/subjects/physics/quantum-physics-quantum-information-and-quantum-computation/quantum-error-correction?format=HB=9780521897877"} ISBN:9780521897877
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.0277"} arxiv:1311.0277
            %li
              %p
                Paul Herringer:
                %em The Toric Code
                (2020) [
                %a{:href => "https://www.physics.rutgers.edu/grad/602/Lectures/JC_Presentations/0419/Intro_Toric_Code.pdf"}> pdf
                ]
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toric_code"} Toric code
            %li
              %p
                errorcorrectionzoo.org:
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://errorcorrectionzoo.org/c/toric"} Toric code
          %p On the problem of its experimental realization:
          %ul
            %li#NussinovOrtiz08
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zohar+Nussinov"}> Zohar Nussinov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerardo+Ortiz"} Gerardo Ortiz
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Autocorrelations and Thermal Fragility of Anyonic Loops in Topologically Quantum Ordered Systems
                %strong 77
                x (2008) Phys. Rev. B 77, 064302 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.77.064302"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.77.064302
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.2717"} arXiv:0709.2717
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.2717"} arXiv:0709.2717
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “the existence of a gap in this system may not protect a finite expectation value of the Toric code operators […] The physical reason behind this result is the proliferation of topological defects (solitons) at any finite
                  = succeed ".\u201D" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %li#VLV21
              %p
                Ruben Verresen, Mikhail D. Lukin, Ashvin Vishwanath:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Prediction of Toric Code Topological Order from Rydberg Blockade
                %strong 11
                (2021) 031005 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevX.11.031005"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevX.11.031005
                ]
              %blockquote
                %p “Unfortunately, the experimental realization of such phases […] has been exceedingly difficult.”
          %div
            %h3#ReferencesAnyonicTopologicalOrderOnTori Anyonic topological order on tori
            %p
              The theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyonic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topologically ordered
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"} quantum materials
              is often discussed assuming periodic
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyon
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            %p
              In fact, the notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
              was introduced already assuming
              = succeed "-shaped" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xiao-Gang+Wen"}> Xiao-Gang Wen
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Vacuum Degeneracy of Chiral Spin State in Compactified Spaces
                  %strong 40
                  7387 (1989) [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.40.7387"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.40.7387
                  ]
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xiao-Gang+Wen"}> Xiao-Gang Wen
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Topological Orders in Rigid States
                  %strong 4
                  239 (1990) [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217979290000139"}> doi:10.1142/S0217979290000139
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://xgwen.mit.edu/sites/default/files/documents/topo.pdf"} pdf
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xiao-Gang+Wen"}> Xiao-Gang Wen
                  , Qian Niu:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Ground state degeneracy of the FQH states in presence of random potential and on high genus Riemann surfaces
                  %strong 41
                  9377 (1990) [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.41.9377"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.41.9377
                  ]
              %li
                %p
                  E. Keski-Vakkuri,
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xiao-Gang+Wen"} Xiao-Gang Wen
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Ground state structure of hierarchical QH states on torus and modular transformation
                  %strong 7
                  4227 (1993) [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217979293003644"}> doi:10.1142/S0217979293003644
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9303155"} arXiv:hep-th/9303155
            %p Further discussion along these lines:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  Zhu-Xi Luo, Yu-Ting Hu, Yong-Shi Wu:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em On Quantum Entanglement in Topological Phases on a Torus
                  %strong 94
                  075126 (2016) [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.94.075126"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.94.075126
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.01777"} arXiv:1603.01777
              %li
                %p
                  Zhuan Li, Roger S. K. Mong:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Detecting topological order from modular transformations of ground states on the torus
                  %strong 106
                  (2022) 235115 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.106.235115"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.106.235115
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.04329"} arXiv:2203.04329
            %p
              Explicit discussion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"} anyons
              on
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tori"} tori
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xiao-Gang+Wen"}> Xiao-Gang Wen
                  , E. Dagotto,
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Fradkin"} Eduardo Fradkin
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Anyons on a torus
                  %strong 42
                  (1990) 6110 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.42.6110"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.42.6110
                  ]
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Iengo"}> Roberto Iengo
                  ,
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+Lechner"} Kurt Lechner
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Quantum mechanics of anyons on a torus
                  %strong 346
                  2–3 (1990) 551-575 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(90)90292-L"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(90)90292-L
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/28316"} spire:28316
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Iengo"}> Roberto Iengo
                  ,
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+Lechner"} Kurt Lechner
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Exact results for anyons on a torus
                  %strong 364
                  3 (1991) 551-583 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(91)90277-5"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(91)90277-5
                  ]
              %li
                %p
                  Yasuhiro Hatsugai, Mahito Kohmoto, Yong-Shi Wu:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Anyons on a torus: Braid group, Aharonov-Bohm period, and numerical study
                  %strong 43
                  (1991) 10761 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.43.10761"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.43.10761
                  ]
              %li
                %p
                  Yutaka Hosotani, Choon-Lin Ho:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Anyons on a Torus
                  %strong 272
                  (1992) 1466–1469 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/1.43444"}> doi:10.1063/1.43444
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9210112"} arXiv:hep-th/9210112
              %li
                %p
                  Choon-Lin Ho, Yutaka Hosotani:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Anyon equation on a torus
                  %strong 07
                  23 (1992) 5797-5831 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217751X92002647"}> doi:10.1142/S0217751X92002647
                  ]
              %li
                %p
                  Ikuo Ichinose, Toshiyuki Ohbayashi:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Exactly soluble model of multispecies anyons and the braid group on a torus
                  %strong 419
                  (1994) 529-552 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(94)90343-3"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(94)90343-3
                  ]
              %li#Kitaev03
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexei+Kitaev"}> Alexei Kitaev
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Fault-tolerant quantum computation by anyons
                  %strong 303
                  1 (2003) 2-30 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0003-4916(02)00018-0"}> doi:10.1016/S0003-4916(02)00018-0
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9707021"} arXiv:quant-ph/9707021
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    (introducing the
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toric+code"} toric code
              %li
                %p
                  Songyang Pu,
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jainendra+K.+Jain"} Jainendra K. Jain
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Composite anyons on a torus
                  %strong 104
                  (2021) 115135 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.104.115135"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.104.115135
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.15705"} arXiv:2106.15705
              %li#Simon23
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+H.+Simon"}> Steven H. Simon
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Anyon Vacuum on a Torus and Quantum Memory
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Topological Quantum
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/academic/product/topological-quantum-9780198886723"} ISBN:9780198886723
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "http://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/SteveSimon/topological2021/TopoBook-Sep28-2021.pdf"} pdf
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "http://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/SteveSimon/topological2021/topocourse2021.html"} webpage
              %li
                %p
                  Shang Liu:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Anyon quantum dimensions from an arbitrary ground state wave function
                  %strong 15
                  (2024) 5134 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-47856-7"}> doi:10.1038/s41467-024-47856-7
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.13235"} arXiv:2304.13235
            %p
              But anyonic states may alternatively be localized in more abstract spaces. Anyons localized not in position space but in “reciprocal momentum space”, namely on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
              of quasi-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momenta"} momenta
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
              in a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystal
            %ul
              %li#SatiSchreiber22
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Anyonic+topological+order+in+TED+K-theory"} Anyonic topological order in TED K-theory
                %strong 35
                03 (2023) 2350001 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0129055X23500010"}> doi:10.1142/S0129055X23500010
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.13563"} arXiv:2206.13563
            %p
              Also proposals to classify (free or interacting)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
              mean to consider them on all base space topologies, including
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tori"} tori
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anton+Kapustin"}> Anton Kapustin
                  \:
                  %em Symmetry Protected Topological Phases, Anomalies, and Cobordisms: Beyond Group Cohomology
                  [
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/1283873"}> spire:1283873
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.1467"} arXiv:1403.1467
                %blockquote
                  %p “Our basic assumption is that a gapped state of matter with short-range interactions can be put on a curved space-time of arbitrary topology […] At short distances a system is usually defined on a regular lattice, with short-range interactions. However, if we allow for disorder, then dislocations in the lattice are possible, and more general triangulations also become possible”
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anton+Kapustin"}> Anton Kapustin
                  \:
                  %em Bosonic Topological Insulators and Paramagnets: a view from cobordisms
                  [
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/1292830"}> spire:1292830
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.6659"} arXiv:1404.6659
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    “SPT phases are usually defined on a spatial lattice, while time may or may not be discretized. In the effective action approach we want to allow space-time to have an arbitrary topology, thus we discretize both space and time and regard the system as being defined on a general triangulation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                    of a
                    = succeed "-dimensional" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi d
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                    = succeed ".\u201D" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              and is implicit also in the proposal of classification via
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+field+theory"} invertible field theory
              of:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+S.+Freed"}> Daniel S. Freed
                  ,
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+J.+Hopkins"} Michael J. Hopkins
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Reflection positivity and invertible topological phases
                  %strong 25
                  (2021) 1165-1330 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2140/gt.2021.25.1165"}> doi:10.2140/gt.2021.25.1165
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.06527"} arXiv:1604.06527
              %li
                %p
                  Kazuya Yonekura:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em On the cobordism classification of symmetry protected topological phases
                  %strong 368
                  (2019) 1121–1173 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-019-03439-y"}> doi:10.1007/s00220-019-03439-y
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.10796"} arXiv:1803.10796
            %p
              The broad idea that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
              is the right language to speak about anyonic topological order is now often stated as if self-evident, e.g. in:
            %ul
              %li#Simon23
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+H.+Simon"}> Steven H. Simon
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Anyons and Topological Quantum Field Theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topological Quantum
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/academic/product/topological-quantum-9780198886723"} ISBN:9780198886723
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/SteveSimon/topological2021/TopoBook-Sep28-2021.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/SteveSimon/topological2021/topocourse2021.html"} webpage
            %p
              Critical commentary on the assumption of non-trivial topology in position space appears in the following (whose authors then suggest that using
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+functorial+field+theory"} extended
              TQFT may ameliorate the problem, p. 2):
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Davide+Gaiotto"}> Davide Gaiotto
                  ,
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theo+Johnson-Freyd"} Theo Johnson-Freyd
                  %em Condensations in higher categories
                  [
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/1736539"}> spire:1736539
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.09566"} arXiv:1905.09566
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    “This relationship between gapped condensed matter systems and TQFTs is perplexing, particularly so if one takes a “global” approach to TQFTs, defining them à la
                    %a{:href => "topological+quantum+field+theory#Atiyah89"} Atiyah 1988
                    in terms of partition functions attached to non-trivial Euclidean space-time manifolds and spaces of states attached to non-trivial space manifolds. From that perspective, matching a given lattice system to a TQFT would require identifying a lot of extra structure to be added to the definition of the lattice system in order to define it on discretizations of non-trivial space manifolds and to define adiabatic evolutions analogous to non-trivial space-time manifolds.”
                  %p “If one takes the information-theoretic perspective that a phase of matter is fully characterized by the local entanglement properties of the ground-state wavefunction of the system, with no reference to a time evolution, then even more work may be needed.”
                  %p “These concerns are not just abstract. Given some phase of matter, in the lab or in a computer, it is hard to extract the data which would pin down the corresponding TQFT, or even know if the TQFT exists. For example, we can hardly place a three-dimensional material on a non-trivial space-manifold. We can only try to simulate that by employing judicious collections of defects in flat space.”
